# Preserve source topology, UVs, materials, textures, pivots, rigs, animations,…

Preserve source topology, UVs, materials, textures, pivots, rigs, animations,
placements, and transforms without artistic edits.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Preserve source topology, UVs, materials, textures, pivots, rigs,
  animations, placements, and transforms without artistic edits.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

World-analysis mesh loading now uses the same strict authored-topology contract
as ordinary FBX mesh loading. Triangle-strip stitching degenerates remain a
deterministic topology conversion performed by strip triangulation, while an
authored triangle-list record with repeated vertex indices fails closed instead
of being silently deleted. A focused regression proves analysis no longer
repairs a malformed triangle list; the complete FBX package, strict FBX Clippy,
and all 54 world-level tests pass. The legacy world-catalog discard-count field
is retained for schema compatibility and is now zero for render meshes.

Intersect collision meshes now follow the same fail-closed rule. Their decoded
format is an explicit count-prefixed triangle index array with no strip/stitch
selector, so repeated-index source triangles are no longer deleted before mesh
construction. A focused collision regression proves the exact malformed source
topology is rejected, and the legacy collision discard count remains zero for
catalog compatibility.

World mesh publication now also preserves source mesh grouping itself. Spatial
distance between disconnected components no longer triggers vertex compaction,
primitive regrouping, or generated independent-object identities. Near and
distant synthetic meshes remain byte-equivalent at the typed mesh boundary,
and the position/UV preservation regression remains green.

Rigid-prop rest-transform baking now applies the inverse-transpose affine basis
to authored normals instead of reusing the position basis. Non-uniform authored
joint scale therefore preserves surface orientation while positions still use
the exact source rest transform; a focused regression proves a diagonal normal
under 2x X scale resolves to the mathematically correct transformed direction.

Skeletal PTRN binding now fails closed when a bound source group carries a
transform property other than the explicitly preserved `TRAN` and `ROT_`
channels. Unsupported authored transform data can no longer disappear from the
export silently; unmatched helper groups remain retained as ignored evidence.

Skeletal animation rest defaults now decompose the authored local matrix before
quaternion recovery, removing scale from the rotation basis while retaining the
exact decomposed translation. A 90-degree Z rest rotation with 2x authored X
scale now recovers the original rotation instead of a scale-distorted angle.

Bound skeletal animation keys now must remain inside the declared clip frame
range. Authored keys beyond the last exported frame fail closed instead of being
silently truncated while interpolation samples only the shorter clip duration.

Skeletal PTRN loading now also models decoder-produced top-level loose channels
and legacy animation extras explicitly. Non-empty payloads fail closed instead
of being silently discarded by Serde while grouped skeletal tracks are exported.

Skeleton loading now rejects joint-control semantics that the current FBX bone
contract cannot represent: nonzero DOF/free-axis/axis selectors and decoded
joint metadata. These authored rig controls can no longer be flattened silently
into an unconstrained parent/child hierarchy.

Whole-character loading now retains decoded composite-effect presence through
the shared binding parser and rejects non-empty effects because the current FBX
character contract cannot preserve their joint/translucency/sort semantics.
Explicit selected-rigid-prop export may still prune unrelated effect branches.

Primitive-group normal attachment now preserves already validated authored
triangle winding instead of swapping the second and third index when geometric
facing disagrees with the summed source vertex normals. Source topology and
source normals therefore remain independent evidence through the FBX domain;
the declared FBX export-root transform remains the orientation conversion
boundary.

A normalized-corpus audit found the removed repair would have changed 327 of
190,162 orientable mesh triangles across 64 primitive groups and 2,741 of
268,870 orientable skin triangles across 211 groups. These are input-evidence
counts, not a claim that every audited component is published. The complete FBX
package and all 1,006 pipeline library tests pass with strict FBX and pipeline
Clippy clean.

Decoded P3D triangle-list groups now require exact triples instead of allowing a
four-index partial list to fall through the generic quad triangulation helper.
Both mesh and skin adapters reject that malformed source before mesh or skin
binding continues, while explicit non-P3D quad callers retain their separate
domain helper contract. The normalized corpus contains 3,055 mesh and 198 skin
`prim_type=0` groups; every one has an index count divisible by three. Focused
red/green regressions cover both adapters and the full FBX and pipeline suites
remain green.

Decoded mesh and skin UV intake now has focused evidence for the existing
fail-closed single-channel contract. Both adapters reject a populated nonzero UV
channel instead of choosing one channel and dropping the rest; they also reject
duplicate channel-zero evidence. The normalized corpus contains 18,148 mesh
primitive groups and 1,462 skin primitive groups. All 19,610 groups carry
exactly one non-empty channel zero, with no nonzero or duplicate channel
identity observed.

Decoded P3D mesh `RENDERSTATUS` is now interpreted according to the schema's
`CastShadow` field instead of being discarded. `MeshAsset` retains the optional
boolean source flag, binary FBX mesh nodes publish declared values as
`SHAR_P3D_CastShadow` source metadata. Values outside `0` or `1` fail
closed.

The normalized corpus contains 14,297 decoded meshes: 14,283 declare `1`, 12
declare `0`, and two omit the chunk. A focused binary regression verifies the
disabled value is encoded as `0`, not merely that the property name exists.

Packed-normal evidence is redundant rather than exclusive in the current
normalized corpus. All 3,642 mesh groups and 1,462 skin groups carrying packed
normals also carry same-length decoded ordinary-normal arrays; no packed-only
group was observed. Across the same 19,610 primitive groups, no decoded tangent,
binormal, or multi-colour field is present. The adapters' strict JSON shape
continues to reject unsupported fields rather than dropping them silently.

Deferred world billboard controller relationships now retain their exact BQG
animation member identity and source ordinal instead of losing the animation
edge when model FBX publication omits billboard runtime semantics. This is
relationship preservation only: the decoded BQG channel payload remains the
source authority and is not flattened into skeletal or static FBX animation.

A focused audit of the 138 linked BQG animations finds 134 at 30 fps and four at
24 fps; all are non-cyclic and have no loose channels or legacy animation
extras. Eight animations contain four named groups, while the other 130 contain
one group. Channel sequences split into 82 standard eight-channel records, 43
width/height-only records, eight four-group repetitions of the standard set,
and five extended records that also carry `ERNG`, `SRNG`, and `ORNG`. Those
source differences remain explicitly deferred for a later faithful billboard
animation representation rather than normalized away.

Deferred world billboards now preserve their source presentation payload
separately from the static inspection planes used by FBX review. Across the 284
current child quads, authored orientation modes are 254 `NOAX`, 19 `YAX`, eight
`AAX`, and three `LYAX`; cutoff modes are 279 `N0NE` and five `DBL`. All current
children enable perspective, while authored distance is zero for 276 children,
`0.65` for seven, and `-0.35` for one.

The current UV-offset range, source range, and edge range are zero for all 284
children, but the v4 catalog still retains those fields rather than inferring a
global default. Ten shader identities and 55 child identities occur across the
260 groups, and no audited presentation float is non-finite. Camera-facing,
cutoff, depth, and BQG animation execution remain deferred to a reviewed native
presentation adapter instead of being flattened into static or skeletal FBX.

Deferred billboard shader evidence now uses the same validated decoded-shader
contract as FBX material resolution while preserving the full source parameter
array. Schema/version, platform shader identity, translucency, vertex masks,
declared parameter count, canonical `TEX`, and exact decoded parameter values
remain available without translating them into runtime material semantics.

Across the current 260 deferred groups, the retained relationships reference ten
shader identities and nine canonical texture tokens. Their 271 shader
occurrences carry 7,017 parameter rows through the catalog boundary; nine of the
11 ambiguous relationship sets differ in non-texture shader payload while none
disagree on `TEX`. Native blend, depth, camera-facing, cutoff, and animation
execution therefore remain deferred instead of being inferred from one selected
shader occurrence.

Replaying the existing cross-package texture authority against the phase-three
index resolves 248 of the 260 deferred billboard texture references without
conflict. The remaining 12 are all level-03 `glow2.bmp` uses: their preferred
terrain scope contains two distinct normalized PNG payloads for the same source
identity. No local billboard shader candidate disagrees about the `TEX` token,
but that agreement does not identify one of the two physical texture payloads.

Both conflicting level-03 textures are independent top-level chunks, and the
terrain shader that names `glow2.bmp` is also an independent top-level chunk.
There is therefore no decoded parent/container edge supporting an occurrence
choice. Texture publication for those deferred billboard semantics remains open
until stronger source or native evidence can disambiguate the relationship.

Deferred billboard provenance now retains the physical texture side of that
open relationship as well as the logical shader token. The same existing scope
preference used for material staging contributes every preferred package/member
occurrence, its source ordinal, and normalized PNG digest to
`shar.world-model-props.v6`; no preferred occurrence is promoted to an authored
relationship by this step.

The 260 current logical billboard texture references retain 300 physical
occurrences. Twenty-two references have multiple preferred occurrences whose
payloads are byte-identical, while the 12 level-03 `glow2.bmp` references retain
their two differing payloads as explicit ambiguity. Runtime texture selection,
material recreation, and native billboard presentation remain deferred rather
than being inferred from this provenance inventory.

The 138 controller-linked BQG clips were rechecked at the decoded channel
boundary. All declared group, channel, and keyed-frame counts agree with their
payloads; keyed frames are strictly increasing and within the declared clip,
and all 934 observed interpolation records use mode 1. Every one of the 138
animation group sequences exactly matches its linked billboard child sequence.

Across those 162 linked animation-group/child pairs, every frame-zero width,
height, distance, UV offset, translation, colour, edge-range, source-range, and
offset-range channel value matches the corresponding decoded static child field
when a frame-zero key exists. However, 49 width/height channels and 38 colour
channels have no frame-zero key, and rotation differs from the static rotation
in 75 of 119 frame-zero cases. That mixed evidence rules out flattening BQG
channel data into static billboard defaults without a reviewed runtime
interpretation.

A dedicated decoded billboard-animation evidence adapter now validates BQG
source payloads independently from skeletal FBX animation. It preserves source
frame span and rate, cyclic state, platform-size records, authored group order,
group ids, declared counts, six observed channel representation families, key
frames, values, and interpolation metadata without sampling or flattening them.
Unsupported top-level payloads, channel semantics, contradictory
counts,
out-of-range or unordered keys, and non-finite floating values fail closed.

The exact deferred-controller relationship set was replayed through that reader:
all 138 linked animations pass, retaining 162 groups and 1,053 channels. World
intake also requires the validated animation-group sequence to equal the linked
billboard-child sequence before provenance is accepted. Their validated payloads
travel with controller provenance in `shar.world-model-props.v7`; this remains
source evidence only and does not claim native camera-facing, cutoff, material,
or BQG runtime execution.

The reader was then replayed against all 1,100 normalized BQG animations. The
corpus contains one platform-size record and one group list per clip; all 8,158
non-visibility channel metadata records use interpolation mode 1. Rotation uses
636 ordinary quaternion channels and 437 packed-quaternion channels, while all
1,108 visibility channels carry a `u16` start state plus ordered raw `u16`
values rather than a decoded boolean-value vector.

The corrected reader accepts 1,098 clips, retaining 1,212 groups and 9,247
channels. Two clips remain rejected because the P3D decoder deliberately renders
non-finite source floats as JSON `null`: one rotation key and one width
sequence. No numeric value is synthesized for those cases. World provenance
advances to
`shar.world-model-props.v8` so visibility is serialized as `raw_values` and
packed rotations remain distinct compressed source evidence.

Vehicle BQG evidence has the same source relationship structure even though its
runtime presentation remains deferred. The current vehicle corpus contains 65
BQG clips across 21 packages; every clip has one controller, one matching quad
group, a composite reference, and exact animation-group-to-billboard-child
ordering. The vehicle catalog now retains those source identities and ordinals
alongside the verbatim effect-animation sidecar.

One vehicle BQG sidecar contains the already-audited non-finite width sequence.
It remains preserved as decoded JSON with `null` values and is not passed to the
skeletal animation reader or repaired by the new relationship metadata. Native
billboard animation semantics therefore remain open rather than being inferred
from the sidecar relationship.

Vehicle texture-effect provenance is now separated from billboard animation
rather than falling through as an untyped sidecar. The five current `TEX_`
animations each have one `TEX` frame controller whose hierarchy resolves
uniquely to a shader; all five decoded animation payloads contain one entity
channel with parameter `TEX_` and no non-finite JSON values.

The vehicle catalog retains that controller-to-shader source relationship and
its exact source ordinals, but the effect JSON remains verbatim. Texture-frame
selection and shader runtime animation remain deferred because this step does
not interpret the entity-channel payload or recreate native material behavior.

Vehicle intake now validates that limited source envelope before publishing a
`TEX_` sidecar. Across all five clips, declared entity-key counts equal
frame and value counts, frames are strictly increasing and inside the clip
span, group identity exactly matches `TEX_` plus the controller-target shader,
and all five interpolation metadata records are version 0/mode 1. The 48 entity
values are
validated only as fixed-width source identities; their runtime texture meaning
remains deliberately uninterpreted.

Those 48 entity references resolve to 12 logical texture identities in their
owning vehicle packages. Sixteen references have one exact physical texture
occurrence, while 32 references have two exact same-name occurrences. Of those
32 ambiguous references, 23 candidate pairs are byte-identical and nine contain
two different normalized PNG payloads.

None of the 32 ambiguous references has a candidate sharing the animation,
controller, or target-shader container. Container ownership therefore cannot
select a physical texture occurrence, and ordinal proximity would be an invented
relationship. The entity channel remains logical source evidence until stronger
source or native semantics can disambiguate those nine differing-payload cases.

The target shader does establish the logical frame-zero state. Each of the five
current target shaders has exactly one canonical `TEX` parameter, and every one
matches the `TEX_` entity-channel key at frame zero. Vehicle intake validates
that equality against the exact target shader source ordinal before preserving
the sidecar, so shader defaults and animation evidence cannot silently disagree.

This still does not assign runtime texture-frame semantics or choose a
duplicated PNG. Three frame-zero logical identities have duplicate physical
occurrences; one pair is byte-identical and two pairs differ in payload. Logical
initial-state agreement is therefore stronger provenance, not physical
occurrence authority.

Vehicle catalog v4 now preserves every package-local physical occurrence behind
those logical `TEX_` entity values without assigning runtime meaning. Across the
five clips, 32 first-use logical reference records retain 48 normalized PNG
occurrences; half the reference records have one occurrence and half have two.
Nine duplicated reference records contain differing payload digests, so all
candidates remain visible rather than being collapsed to one texture frame.

Occurrence provenance comes from the normalized component ledger that directly
addresses each hashed PNG: physical member id, source component ordinal, and
SHA-256 are retained together. The phase-three package index preserves the same
physical coordinate rather than introducing another ordinal domain. All 1,479
current car texture members match one ledger PNG by exact path and carry the
same component ordinal.

That one-to-one mirror is occurrence identity, not relationship authority. A
shader still exposes only its logical texture string, so the phase-three member
cannot select between two same-name physical occurrences without another source
edge.

The ambiguity is active on the current vehicle render path rather than only
sidecar debt. All 11 quad groups using the five `TEX_` target shaders pass the
strict billboard-source contract and are composite-reachable, so their shaders
participate in retained material resolution. Three frame-zero logical texture
identities have duplicate local ledger occurrences; the material adapter rejects
all three instead of selecting one, including the byte-identical duplicate pair.

Two of those duplicate pairs contain different PNG payloads, so weakening that
fail-closed behavior would invent source semantics. End-to-end publication for
those three target materials therefore remains blocked pending stronger source
or native occurrence authority even though v4 can represent every candidate.

The vehicle-wide texture audit is larger than the five `TEX_` effects. Across
786 shader texture bindings that are actually referenced by retained mesh or
billboard geometry, 402 bindings have two same-package normalized texture
occurrences. Those duplicates span 54 car packages; 286 pairs are
byte-equivalent and 116 pairs contain different PNG payloads.

Vehicle texture authority therefore now derives logical identity from each
normalized texture ledger row while retaining its exact physical PNG path,
member id, source component ordinal, owning package evidence, and SHA-256. The
current 89 car ledgers provide complete one-to-one coverage for all 1,479
normalized PNGs, with no path or source-ordinal gaps.

This matters for collision-renamed payloads: 461 texture rows have physical file
names whose normalized lookup key differs from the authored ledger identity,
and 420 of those paths carry an explicit collision suffix. Authority lookup now
groups those physical occurrences by authored identity before applying the same
payload-equivalence rule; it does not infer which differing occurrence a shader
meant.

The remaining 116 differing-payload used bindings therefore stay fail-closed.
The new occurrence evidence improves diagnostics and future sidecar authority,
but does not turn source ordinal, filename, or byte equivalence into unsupported
runtime texture-selection semantics.

A current-route replay found zero used shader bindings whose selected material
package lacks the logical texture and therefore needs cross-package fallback.
The ledger-key correction is still required for that authority boundary, but it
does not bypass the 402 local duplicate relationships that fail before fallback
is considered.

All 402 duplicate used bindings share the same top-level layout: two self-owned
texture chunks with parent ordinal 0 precede a self-owned shader chunk with
parent ordinal 0, and exactly one texture path receives the extractor's
generated ordinal collision suffix. The later texture is always nearer the
shader, but the source shader texture-parameter schema stores only `Param` plus
a string `Value`.
It has no occurrence ordinal or handle, so proximity cannot safely select one
physical payload.

The FBX material adapter now exposes a separate exact texture-occurrence
boundary for consumers that later obtain real relationship authority. A caller
may supply one exact package-ledger PNG occurrence together with an exact shader
path; the physical path selects the occurrence, while the shader's authored
`TEX` identity controls the staged PNG name. Unpublished files and shaders with
no texture parameter are rejected. Ordinary material resolution is unchanged
and still rejects ambiguous same-name ledger candidates.

Community tooling evidence narrows ordering semantics without resolving that
ambiguity. Donut Team guidance for Lucas' Pure3D Editor requires a texture chunk
to appear above the shader that references it and describes shared textures as
resources found by the shader's logical texture name. All 402 current duplicate
vehicle bindings already have both same-name texture occurrences above their
shader. The public ordering evidence therefore cannot distinguish the base path
from the ordinal-qualified collision path.

`shar.vehicle-catalog.v5` now carries that verified occurrence coordinate into
TEX sidecar evidence. Every retained physical texture occurrence requires one
unique phase-three `p3d-texture` member whose exact package path and source
chunk ordinal both match the normalized ledger row. The stable package-member id
is
serialized alongside the physical member id, component ordinal, and PNG digest.

This is still non-selecting provenance. Multiple same-name occurrences remain
separate v5 records. Neither the phase-three id nor its ordinal is treated as a
shader-to-texture relationship. The new exact-occurrence FBX boundary can use
one physical member only after another source-backed consumer establishes that
relationship authority.

`shar.world-model-props.v9` now gives every retained deferred billboard
texture occurrence the same stable phase-three physical coordinate. A whole
terrain-world audit covers 149 packages and 5,866 normalized texture ledger
rows: every row
matches exactly one phase-three `p3d-texture` member by exact component path,
every member carries the same source chunk ordinal as its ledger row, and there
are zero missing, duplicate-path, or unrepresented occurrences.

World shared-texture authority now requires that exact path-and-ordinal join
while it ingests a ledger occurrence, then retains the phase-three
package-member id
alongside the existing package id, subcategory, normalized member id, source
ordinal, and PNG digest. A focused regression proves that a matching path with a
wrong ordinal, or a matching ordinal with a wrong collision path, fails closed.

This strengthens physical occurrence identity only. The existing scope
preference and payload-equivalence rules are unchanged, so the 12 differing
`glow2.bmp` preferred occurrence sets remain unresolved and all same-name
physical peers stay visible rather than being promoted to an authored shader
relationship.

`shar.world-model-props.v10` extends the same exact physical coordinate to
deferred billboard shader occurrences. A separate whole terrain-world audit
covers 9,991 normalized shader ledger rows across the same 149 packages: every
row matches exactly one phase-three `p3d-shader` member by component path and
source ordinal, with zero missing, duplicate-path, or unrepresented rows.

World inventory now builds a package-local phase-three shader map keyed only by
that exact path-and-ordinal pair. Every same-name shader occurrence retained for
a deferred billboard must resolve through the map, and v10 serializes the stable
package-member id beside the normalized shader member id and decoded authored
shader payload. The existing same-name occurrence list remains
source-ordinal ordered and non-selecting; this adds provenance without choosing
a shader winner.

The same audit method identifies the next bounded world provenance gap without
changing v10 behavior. Across the 149 terrain-world packages, all 1,091
`quad_group` rows match `p3d-mesh` phase-three members by exact path and
ordinal,
all 4,032 `animation` rows match `p3d-animation` members, and all 2,802
